course: dictionary
lesson: 13573
title: The glow of incandescence
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Incandescence is a noun. It means the light that something gives off because it is very hot. A metal wire, a flame or a red-hot coal can shine this way. «The old light bulb glowed because electricity heated its thin wire until it shone with incandescence.» The bulb is not glowing because it is cold. The heat is what makes it give out light.
explain: Writers also use incandescence for a strong, bright glow, or for a burning feeling inside someone. «Her anger burned with an incandescence that no one could ignore.» Anger does not give off real light, so here the word means a fierce, burning feeling. Ask yourself: is the thing really hot and glowing, or is the writer describing a strong feeling?
explain: Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence. «The lava gave off a bright incandescence as it ran down the mountain.» If you swap in "the light given off by something very hot", the sentence still makes sense. That is a good sign you have the right meaning.
fact: Incandescence means the light given off by something that is very hot.
fact: «Incandescent bulb» is the name for an old-fashioned bulb that works by heating a thin wire until it glows.
fact: The Sun, flames, red-hot coals and lava all shine by incandescence.
fact: In stories, «incandescence» can also mean a strong, bright glow or a burning strong feeling.
fact: Incandescence is an uncountable noun, so we say «the incandescence», not «an incandescence» or «incandescences».
exercise: Mia says, «The lava gave off a fierce incandescence as it poured down the hill.» What does incandescence mean here? | A loud noise from a volcano | The cold, dark shade under a rock | *The light given off by something very hot | A sticky sort of mud
exercise: «The electric wire glowed white with incandescence.» Why does the wire shine? | Because it is wet | Because someone painted it white | Because it is very cold | *Because it is very hot
exercise: Which of these things shines by incandescence? | *A red-hot coal | A wooden chair in the dark | A sleeping cat | A cold stone at night
test: «Her courage burned with an incandescence that inspired the whole team.» In this sentence, incandescence means | A sleepy yawn | A thin metal wire | *A strong, burning feeling | A cold, quiet mood
test: Which phrase names an old-fashioned light that works by incandescence? | *An incandescent bulb | A cold drink | A wooden spoon | A digital screen
exam: Which sentence uses incandescence correctly? | *The incandescence of the lava lit up the dark valley | The incandescence of the friendly dog was very sleepy | The incandescence of the cold puddle made it freeze solid | I ate some incandescence for breakfast
